% ============================================================================
% plate-boundaries — the three types of tectonic plate boundary and how the
% plates move at each one (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH-SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of plate tectonics, and a rung an EARTH-SCIENCE
% agent reasons UP from: the rigid outer shell of the Earth is broken into
% plates, and everywhere two plates meet the plates are doing one of just THREE
% things — pulling apart, coming together, or sliding side-by-side. That single
% distinction is what predicts whether you get a mid-ocean ridge, a mountain
% belt / volcanic arc, or a strike-slip fault. Before you can reason about a
% rift, a subduction zone, or the San Andreas fault you must first know WHICH of
% the three boundary types you are looking at. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? boundary_motion(divergent, $Motion)      % rip_apart
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `boundary_motion(boundary, motion)` carrying the table's citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% motion WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these three boundary types (it never invents a
% motion).
%
% Each of the three plate-boundary types and how the source says the plates move
% there, as a little truth table:
%
%     boundary type   how the plates move (source's word)   a beginner's cue
%     -------------   -----------------------------------   ----------------------
%     divergent       rip_apart                             plates move away — a rift
%     convergent      subducts                              one plate dives beneath
%     transform       slide_past                            plates slide side-by-side
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a motion and recall its boundary type:
%
%     ? boundary_motion($Boundary, slide_past)   % transform
%
% A word that is NOT one of these three boundary types — `equator`, `hotspot`,
% `rift` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a boundary recall ABSTAINS on it rather
% than inventing a motion. That honest-abstention property is what makes the
% table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the boundary→motion
% pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `motion` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be the
% word the source EXPLICITLY uses for the movement at that boundary — never one
% the source does not support. (Where the everyday word differs from the
% source's word — the source says plates "rip apart", not merely "move apart";
% it says one plate "subducts" beneath the other rather than "collide"; it says
% plates "slide ... past" one another — the atom uses the source's own word, so
% every value stays literally checkable against the quoted span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every boundary→motion pair
% below was read out of a fetched U.S. National Park Service (NPS) geology page,
% and any pair whose motion could not be verified there would have been
% dropped). Each pair, with the primary source it was copied from and the
% verbatim span that states it:
%
%   divergent → rip_apart
%     https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/plate-tectonics-types-of-plate-boundaries.htm
%     (National Park Service — U.S. Department of the Interior)
%     "Plates rip apart at a divergent plate boundary, causing volcanic activity
%      and shallow earthquakes."
%
%   convergent → subducts
%     https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/plate-tectonics-types-of-plate-boundaries.htm
%     (National Park Service — U.S. Department of the Interior)
%     "At a convergent plate boundary, one plate dives (\"subducts\") beneath the
%      other, resulting in a variety of earthquakes and a line of volcanoes on
%      the overriding plate."
%
%   transform → slide_past
%     https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/plate-tectonics-types-of-plate-boundaries.htm
%     (National Park Service — U.S. Department of the Interior)
%     "Transform plate boundaries are where plates slide laterally past one
%      another, producing shallow earthquakes but little or no volcanic
%      activity."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the NPS statement that fixes the
% first row (divergent → rip_apart). Every OTHER row's per-fact source and
% verbatim span is listed above, so each motion remains independently checkable.
% All sources are the U.S. National Park Service, a primary U.S. government
% (Department of the Interior) teaching resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table boundary_motion {
    columns boundary, motion

    row (divergent, rip_apart)
    row (convergent, subducts)
    row (transform, slide_past)

    source "Plates rip apart at a divergent plate boundary, causing volcanic activity and shallow earthquakes."
    locator "https://www.nps.gov/subjects/geology/plate-tectonics-types-of-plate-boundaries.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
